              },
              {
                ":answer/id": "73176b39-7777-5104-a75d-1270b3419b9c",
                ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
                ":answer/value": "\\frac{121}{113}"
              },
              {
                ":answer/id": "5e052e7c-8d50-5903-aa4d-c36ac432b3ee",
                ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
                ":answer/value": "\\frac{11\\sqrt{113}}{113}"
              }
            ],
            ":answer-field/correct": [
              ":answer/id",
              "5e052e7c-8d50-5903-aa4d-c36ac432b3ee"
            ]
          }
        ],
        ":question/worked-solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186110756-draft/plots/eede830b274be0b969524b75da6809890207306f8f7ac7d4541afe929c8e20ae.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=cos\u2061\\theta ,\\,y=sin\u2061\\theta\n$$\n\nWe're given that $y=\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{11}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\nsin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{11}.\n$$\n\nWe can use the Pythagorean identity to find $cos\u2061\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta +{cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1 \\\\ {cos}^{2}\u2061\\theta  & =1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta  \\\\ cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{sin}^{2}\u2061\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $sin\u2061\\theta =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{11}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}cos\u2061\\theta  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-{(\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{11})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{1-\\frac{8}{121}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{113}{121}} \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{11}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe select the positive answer since $cos\u2061\\theta$ is positive in the first quadrant. So, $cos\u2061\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{11}.$\n\nKnowing that $sec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{cos\u2061\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\nsec\u2061\\theta =\\frac{1}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{11})}=\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{113}}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we rationalize the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}sec\u2061\\theta  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{113}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{\\sqrt{113}}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{113}}{\\sqrt{113}} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\sqrt{113}}{113} \\\\  & =\\frac{11\\sqrt{113}}{113}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard"
      },
